## 1. Introduction
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are nanoporous polymers, consisting of inorganic metal centres linked together by organic ligands [1]. These compounds have received special attention because of their unique physical and chemical properties that make them applicable in different fields such as engineering [2], medicine [3] and environment [4]. Since synthesis conditions greatly affect the properties of these compounds, it is specifically important to select a suitable synthesis method that produces samples with homogenous morphology, small size distribution and high thermal stability as well as surface area so that the properties could lead to extensive applications in different fields [5].
MOFs are made in different methods such as hydrothermal [6], solvothermal [7], electrochemical [8], and slow diffusion techniques [9]. In these conventional methods, reactions are carried out in long periods and, in some cases, high temperatures and energies are needed for the progress of the reaction so that it is difficult to control synthesis conditions. Also, the products synthesized in these methods do not have desirable physicochemical properties [10]. Recently, rapid methods like ultrasound [11] and reverse micelle [12] have been used, which could facilitate the performance of the reactions and produce MOF samples in mild conditions. Ultrasound synthesis is a simple, rapid, low cost, environmentally-friendly and safe method that can be employed for acceleration of the process. When high-intensity ultrasonic irradiations $(20\mathrm{kHz}{-}10\mathrm{MHz})$ ) contact with a liquid, shock waves lead to acoustic cavitation in the medium [13]. In the ultrasound method, different parameters are used to control various properties of products [14]. Many researchers have investigated the chemical or physical effects of ultrasonic irradiation on several properties of MOFs [15]. In reverse micelle (RM) method, reverse micelles organized in an organic medium are served as nanoreactors for the formation of nanostructures [16]. A typical RM method for synthesis of MOFs involves the following steps: a) surfactant is dissolved in an organic solvent; b) an aqueous solution containing metal and ligand precursors is added to the surfactant solution to prepare reverse micelles acting as nanoreactors; c) MOF grows inside the micelles through the reaction of the metal precursor and ligand [12]. Generally, each of methods (ultrasound or reverse micelle methods) result in MOFs with improved properties such as thermal stability and surface area in comparison to other methods. However, the properties of the products are still not ideal for industrial applications [17]. It seems that combination of ultrasound and reverse micelle can considerably enhance the properties. According to our search, there are no reports on the use of ultrasound assisted reverse micelle (UARM)
for the synthesis of MOFs.
Similar to the importance of selecting a suitable method that could influence different properties of MOFs, it is specifically significant to select and introduce a new sample of MOF as the starting point for extensive research and finding its different applications. Thorium, an important and first member of 5f-series elements, is known to form complexes in its $^{+4}$ oxidation state resulted in different coordination numbers [18]. Several complexes of thorium have been found to be useful in widespread applications, such as sensors, electrodes, nuclear industry, pathological applications, and material fields extensively [19]. Additionally, the formation of nanocrystals with thorium is of major interest in nanomedicine [20]. There are a few reports on the synthesis of Th-MOFs in literature [21]. On the other hand, the distinctive properties of MOFs and various applications of thorium nanostructures make the synthesis of Th-MOFs attractive. Furthermore, it is possible to produce MOFs with desirable properties for different applications. Synthesis conditions determine the properties of MOFs.
Experimental design can be applied to achieve the conditions in which an MOF with desirable properties is produced. In previous works, the effects of synthesis conditions on the properties of MOFs have been investigated using the conventional experiment design methods, which increase the number of experiments [22] and ignore the interactions between the factors [23]. However, using a scientific experimental design does not impose such a limitation, but also lead to systematic studies. Therefore, the systematic study of the process could be as important as selecting a suitable synthesis method and introducing a new
MOF sample.
For the first time, we synthesized new Th-MOF nanostructures using suitable precursors and UARM method. Also, we synthesized Th-MOF with conventional RM method. Thermo-gravimetric analysis (TGA), differential scanning calorimetry (DSC), X-ray diffraction (XRD), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), CHNS/O elemental analysis, and Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area analysis were used for the characterization of the synthesized MOFs. The synthesis parameters were scientifically designed by $2^{\mathrm{k-1}}$ factorial. The effects of these parameters on the properties of the final products were also studied with analysis of variance (ANOVA). Finally, response surface methodology (RSM) was used to develop the formation of Th-MOF samples with the enhanced properties.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials and instrumentations
Thorium (IV) nitrate pentahydrate (MW: $570.13\mathrm{g/mol}$ , $99.90\%\right)$ , and 2, 6-pyridine dicarboxylic acid (MW: $167.12\mathrm{g/mol}$ , $99.80\%$ ) were purchased from Sigma Aldrich (St. Loius, MO, USA). Surfactant agent as sodium dodecyl sulfate (MW: $288.37\mathrm{g/mol}$ , $99.99\%$ and an oil phase as n- hexane (d: $0.6548~\mathrm{g/ml}$ , $99.70\%\$ were purchased from Merck (Darmstadt, Germany). All materials used in this study were used as received, without further purification. TGA and DSC were turned from room temperature to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a heating rate of $10\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ under an argon atmosphere using SCINCO thermal gravimeter S-100 (Seoul, Korea) and Netzsch QMS403C (Selb, Germany), respectively. XRD patterns were obtained in reflection mode using a powder X-ray diffractometer (X’Pert MPD, PANanalytical, Cu Ka radiation, $\uplambda=0.154\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{,}$ between $10^{\prime\prime}$ and $90^{\prime\prime}$ (2-Theta) with a step width of $0.010^{\prime\prime}$ . For SEM analysis (Philips xl30), the Th-MOF nanostructures were coated with $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{Pd}$ using a Denton Vacuum Desk II) New Jersey, USA (The coated samples were then imaged using a LEO 1500 with a field emission gun operating at $12\mathrm{keV}$ . The FT-IR spectra of the ThMOF samples have been recorded between 4000 and $500\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ as KBr pellets on SHIMADZU FT-IR 8400 Spectrometer (Kyoto, Japan). Total carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, and oxygen of samples were measured on sub-samples in PerkinElmer 2400 CHNS/O Elemental Analyzer (Massachusetts, USA). Pore textural properties including surface area and porosities were measured with an $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption technique (Belsorp mini II (Osaka, Japan)) at 77 K.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Th-MOFs
## 2.2.1. Reverse micelle (RM) method
In a typical reverse micelle procedure, a solution of thorium (IV) nitrate pentahydrate $_{(0.068~g}$ , $0.120\mathrm{mmol})$ and 2, 6-pyridine dicarboxylic acid $(0.040\ \mathrm{g},\ 0.239\ \mathrm{mmol})$ in $21~\mathrm{mL}$ of double-distilled water was prepared. The reaction solution was then added to a mixture solution containing $0.077\mathrm{{mmol}}$ of sodium dodecyl sulfate $_{(0.022g)}$ and $\mathrm{8\mL}$ of n-hexane in a $150\mathrm{mL}$ Pyrex tube. The resulting mixture was then stirred at $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . After $90\mathrm{min}$ , the silvery crystals were isolated by repeating centrifugation and washed with dimethylformamide (DMF) three times to eliminate the excess reagents.
## 2.2.2. Ultrasound assisted reverse micelle (UARM) method
Th-MOF synthesis using UARM method was as follows: the solution obtained in the previous step and using RM method (Section 2.2.1) was entered into the ultrasound bath and undergoes optimal conditions of ultrasound irradiation (ultrasound duration of $21\mathrm{min}$ , temperature of $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and ultrasound power of 175 W). After $30\mathrm{min}$ , the silvery crystals of Th-MOF were formed that were separated after centrifugation similar to reverse micelle method and then washed with DMF. The simple route of Th-MOF synthesis is displayed in Fig. 1.
## 2.2.3. $2^{K-1}$ factorial design
To design the experiments as well as optimize the processes, $2^{\mathrm{k-1}}$ factorial design was employed. This method refers to design with $\pmb{k}$ factors where each factor has only three levels $(-1,0$ and $+1$ ). Box Behnken design (BBD) is a simpler design from the theoretical statistical viewpoint, and would lead to acquire usually simpler model than other related models [24]. Generally, the predictability of BBD is case dependent. However, BBD requires less runs of experiments and fewer treatment combinations in comparison to other similar methods such central composite design especially for less number of parameter [25]. Conventional and classic methods require a large number of experiments and are time-consuming. Additionally, the combination of all variables and their values is not countable. In fact, the use of the $2^{K-1}$ factorial design could help find the best experimental conditions and perform targeted experiments with fewer repetitions. The extent of factors is determined based on the data obtained from the experiments [26]. In this work, we needed a statistical model to analyse the differences among groups; according to previous similar works, we used ANOVA based on well-known $P_{\nu a l u e}$ . This statistical method could satisfy entirely in order to show the significant terms $(P_{\nu a l u e}~<~0.050)$ and exclude the insignificant terms $(P_{\nu a l u e}>0.050)$ as a Pareto chart. It would guarantee to reach a qualified and favourable model that enables us to discuss whether each term introduced in this work covers the interaction of variable factors or not [15,27].
## 2.3. Response surface methodology (RSM)
RSM is a technique for optimization of process parameters. Using RSM plots, it is possible to optimize the important parameters to achieve the most desirable percentage elongation [28]. In addition, the response surface optimization is used to determine the optimal synthesis parameters [29].
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Thermal analysis
Fig. 2 demonstrates the weight changes of the Th-MOF samples obtained from TG curve and DSC analysis. According to the data obtained from TGA, in both RM and UARM methods, the samples exhibited a weight reduction in six steps and the pure products were resulted from the thermal decomposition of the micelle components. Furthermore, TGA showed that use of UARM method resulted in the production of Th-MOF sample with higher thermal stability than those obtained from RM method ( $354^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ vs. $242^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . DSC data confirmed the TGA results; considerable weight change of the samples occurred in six separate steps. According to DSC analyses, the required energy for the decomposition of the components in each step could be calculated [30]. Since all the peaks observed in DSC were related to the weight reduction of the sample components, it was concluded that no phase changes occurred in the Th-MOF samples within the above thermal range. The thermodynamic results obtained from TGA and DSC are shown in Table 1.
## 3.2. Crystallinity
The XRD patterns of the Th-MOF samples prepared by means of RM and UARM methods are showed in Fig. 3. According to the data obtained from these patterns, the sample synthesized by RM method had sharp peaks indicating the large size of the crystals produced in this method (based on the Debye–Scherrer equation). However, use of UARM method broadened the peaks, which proved the nanometric dimensions of the Th-MOF sample. In both RM and UARM methods, the diffraction patterns at the approximate 2ɵ angles of 26, 34, 42, 54, 57, 69, 71, 74, and 77 are related to the crystal planes of (2 0 0), (2 2 0), (3 1 1), (2 2 0), (2 2 2), (2 2 0), (4 0 0), (3 3 1), and (4 2 0), respectively, which is excellently consistent with all of the peaks can be indexed to the cubic crystal system of pure complex of $\mathrm{C_{21}H_{11}N_{3}O_{12}T h}$ . Except the difference in the widening rate of the peaks, which was representative of the size of crystals, as well as the differences in the density of the samples $(1.96~\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ in RM and $0.89\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ in UARM methods), other crystallographic data of the Th-MOF samples synthesized by RM and UARM methods were similar (Table 2).
## 3.3. Morphology and size distribution
Fig. 4 shows the SEM micrographs of the Th-MOF samples synthesized by two different methods. The sample synthesized by RM shows non-uniform size distribution and its morphology is non-homogenous. In this method, the particles tend to agglomeration, which indicates their instability. This issue could reduce their surface area and decrease porosity in final product. However, in the samples obtained from UARM method, the mean size of the particles is $27\mathrm{nm}$ and their morphology is completely uniform so that there is no evidence for the agglomeration or aggregation of their structures. Additionally, the formation of the pores is properly displayed in the SEM image related to Th-MOF sample synthesized by UARM method. This property is in agreement with the data obtained from BET technique (Section 3.5).
## 3.4. FTIR spectra
Fig. 5 depicts the FTIR spectra of the Th-MOF samples synthesized by RM and UARM methods. In both samples, the absorption band at $3212.5\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ may be attributed to the coordinated water in the products. The peak appeared at $3009.1\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is ascribed to the stretching vibration of aromatic $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ . The peak observed around $1440.7\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ confirms the presence of –COO– groups of the ionized ligand and the peak around $1299.9\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is due to CeN bonds. The absorption band at $971.2\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is assigned to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibrations of aliphatic CH and the peak at $594.3\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is assigned to the $\mathrm{Th}{-}0$ bonds. Based on the results of FTIR spectra and considering the different configurations of the ligand [14], the mechanism illustrated in Fig. 6 is proposed for the synthesis of Th-MOF. Steps of mechanism are summarized as follows (in all steps, to avoid congested structures, the water molecules are ignored; however, these molecules are reported in the final structure): First, 2, 6-pyridine dicarboxylic acid (ligand)
attacks the thorium metal ion through its OH group (A). Then, in order to achieve stability, the carboxylic group loses its $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ and the nitrate absorbs this $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ and is converted into a good leaving group $(B)$ . As a result, the nitrate is hydrogenated and leaves the structure $(C)$ . Since the intramolecular attack is prioritized to the intermolecular one (occurred faster), the ligand is connected to the metal at the other end of the carboxylic group $(D)$ . In steps $\pmb{{\cal E}}$ , the $c$ process is repeated (in order to achieve stability, the carboxylic group loses $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ and the hydrogen ion is absorbed by the nitrate). In the next step, as the result of the attack by the nitrogen non-bonding electron pair of the pyridine ring, the hydrated nitrate leaves the structure, which leads to nitrogen instability $(F)$ . Consequently, in order for stability, nitrogen attracts the formed bond and creates an empty site so that another 2, 6-pyridine dicarboxylic acid can be connected to the thorium metal $(G)$ . Afterwards, the 2, 6-pyridine dicarboxylic acid connected to the metal loses the hydrogen connected to its carboxylic group and, then, this freed hydrogen is absorbed by the nitrate and converts it to a suitable leaving group $(H)$ . Again, the non-bonding electron pair of the ligand attacks the metal ion and the nitric acid leaves the structure $(I)$ . Then, in order to eliminate the positive charge, nitrogen absorbs the formed bond and creates an empty site for the attack of the ligand $(J)$ . Due to the steric effect, the intermolecular attack is preferred to the intramolecular one. Therefore, another 2, 6-pyridine dicarboxylic acid attacks the metal ion, which leads to the formation of the bonds $(\pmb{K})$ . Formation of such bonds is continued in steps $\pmb{L}$ and $\pmb{M}$ so that, in step $\pmb{N}$ , a unit of the metal -organic network with the metal-to-ligand molar ratio of 1:3 is formed. Expansion of this unit leads to the formation of the Thorium metal–organic framework (Fig. 7). In addition, the suggested chemical formula of Th-MOF samples is further confirmed by elemental analysis measurements (Fig. 8).
## 3.5. Textural properties
Fig. 9 shows the nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms of the Th-MOF samples synthesized under optimal conditions of both RM and UARM methods. According to the classical adsorption isotherms, the size distribution of the pores is typically micro in RM method (similar to the first-type isotherm), while the adsorption/desorption behaviours of the sample synthesized by UARM method is like the fifth-type of the classical isotherms [31], which indicates the meso nature of the pore size distribution. Furthermore, calculations of the nitrogen adsorption isotherm indicated that the average pore size in the RM-synthesized sample was $0.7{\mathrm{nm}}$ while, the average pore size in the UARM-synthesized sample was $2.8\mathrm{nm}$ . On the other hand, according to the data obtained from nitrogen adsorption/desorption technique, use of UARM could cause the Th-MOF to have higher surface area than RM method $(2.02\times10^{3}\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ vs. $1.69\times10^{3}\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ .
## 3.6. Experimental design
The results obtained from different analyses demonstrated that in UARM method compared with RM method, Th-MOF samples with more desirable properties were synthesized so that particles had more uniform distribution, smaller size, and higher thermal stability. Furthermore, the surface area was higher, and the pore size distribution was meso. Therefore, UARM method was selected as an ideal method for the synthesis of Th-MOF and $2^{\mathrm{k-1}}$ factorial was utilized to scientifically design the process [28]. Also, $2^{\mathrm{k-1}}$ factorial design was performed to evaluate the effects of the parameters of UARM method on Th-MOF properties including size distribution (SD), thermal stability (TS), and surface area (SA). These parameters were surfactant amount (A), ultrasound duration (B), temperature (C), and ultrasound power (D). Each factor was present at three levels (Table 3). The design matrix and the response data obtained from two replicates of the experiments are shown in Table 4 (18 runs were made in a random order) [32].
Fig. 10 shows that under different synthesis conditions, including different amounts of surfactant and different values of ultrasound parameters (duration, temperature, and power), Th-MOF samples had different particle size distributions and morphologies so that sample e had minimum particle size as well as more uniform morphology, while sample $\pmb{b}$ had bulk particle size distribution and the particles were very instable. As a result of such instability, as seen in Fig. 10b, the particles were aggregated together, which led to the agglomerated final structure. Fig. 11 shows that in level e, Th-MOF sample had the highest thermal stability, and the rate of this stability varied depending on the synthesis conditions so that sample b had minimum thermal stability. In all the samples, the thermal behaviours followed a specific pattern, which included vanishing the adsorbed water, evaporation of the water trapped in the network, micelle disintegration, and creation of the pure form of the products. The difference between these patterns was due to the difference in temperatures at which such variations occurred. Fig. 12 represents the nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms, based on which the isotherms of samples e and $\pmb{g}$ were similar to the fifth-type of the classical isotherms, but the adsorption/desorption behaviours of samples $\begin{array}{r}{\boldsymbol{h},i,}\end{array}$ and c were similar to the first-type. Furthermore, samples $\pmb{d}$ and $f$ had adsorption/desorption behaviours similar to the secondtype (serious inconsistencies between the adsorbent and adsorbate), while the isotherms of samples $\pmb{b}$ and $\textbf{\em a}$ were similar to the fourth-type of the classical isotherms of nitrogen [33]. Values of surface area obtained by BET technique are reported in Table 4, according to which samples e and b had the maximum and minimum surface areas, respectively. Furthermore, the values of the surface area of samples synthesized under different conditions were varied. In the present research, Th-MOF samples were synthesized for the first time using a novel method so that the values of particle size, thermal stability, and surface area obtained under optimal conditions were significantly improved compared with the other MOF samples that had been previously synthesized [34,35]. Such a difference could be attributed to the selection of the type of MOF and use of an appropriate synthesis method. Besides, it is of special importance to conduct systematic studies in order to control various properties of Th-MOF samples under different conditions as well as effect of synthesis parameters and also to predict a product with ideal properties.
## 3.7. Ultrasound assisted reverse micelle (UARM) effects
In order to assay a normal distribution, measuring how closely the points on a normal probability plot conform to a straight line is necessary. Studying residuals (the difference between the observed value of the dependent variable and the predicted value) is a key term in interpreting models. For a good model, the residuals should be randomly and normally distributed [28]. Probability plots of the residuals for size distribution, thermal stability, and surface area of Th-MOF samples synthesized under different conditions of UARM are showed in Fig. 13. One can check the independence, especially of time related effects with the residuals versus order (time order of data collection) plots. A positive or a negative correlation means that the assumption is violated [36]. Based on Fig. 13, the plots do not reveal any pattern, and the independence assumption is satisfied. In the present study, the significance level, also denoted as $\scriptstyle{\pmb{\alpha}}_{\nu a l u e s}$ was selected equal to 0.050 (default). Hence, by comparing these values with the $P_{\nu a l u e s}$ (a number between 0 and 1 which is used to weigh the strength of the data validity) obtained for each factor after designing the experiments and interpreting by the ANOVA, it was concluded that if $P_{\nu a l u e}$ was less than $\scriptstyle\alpha_{\nu a l u e},$ the factor would affect the response and, if it was higher than $\mathbf{q_{value}},$ the factor would have no significant effect on the properties (responses). The complete analyses of variance for size distribution (SD), thermal stability (TS), and surface area (SA) of Th-MOF samples synthesized under different conditions of UARM method are summarized in Fig. 14. Results showed that surfactant content (A), ultrasound duration (B), temperature (C), and ultrasound power (D) were important factors because all of them had $P_{\nu a l u e s}$ of 0.000 or close to 0.000. So, they would have a significant impact on particle size distribution, thermal stability, and surface area. Regarding the ANOVA results, since the interaction between the factors had very small $P_{\nu a l u e s},$ the interaction between the ultrasound and reverse micelle parameters was concluded to affect various properties of Th-MOF samples. Fig. 14 presents the ANOVA results producing the Pareto chart of the main and interaction effects. Pareto charts can be used to highlight the most important parameters among a (typically large) set of factors. Using this chart, the vital parameters of the process are characterized [37]. The determination coefficient $(\mathbb{R}^{2})$ of the model was found to be 0.999, with no significant lack of fit. The $F_{\nu a l u e}$ of 5432 implied that the model was highly significant. The results indicated that the model used to fit response variable was significant $(P_{\nu a l u e}~<~0.010)$ ) and adequate to represent the relationship between the responses and the independent variables. Also the large R-adjusted value indicated a good relationship between the experimental data and the fitted model [38].
Regarding the ANOVA results and their generalization to various obtained properties, the designs performed by $2^{\mathrm{K}-1}$ factorial could be analysed. Based on the data obtained from SEM, TGA-DSC, and BET, it seemed that level e had the best conditions for the synthesis of the ThMOF samples. In this level, values of the synthesis parameters (surfactant content, ultrasound duration, temperature, and ultrasound power) were in optimal conditions and would lead to the synthesis of the sample with maximum thermal stability, uniform morphology, minimum particle size, and maximum surface area. Moreover, FTIR (Fig. 15) showed that in this level (e), all of the Th-MOF bonds were formed. Also, in level $\pmb{b}$ , the particles were strictly agglomerated and their sizes were in the bulk form (Fig. 10). Furthermore, in this level, according to the FTIR spectrum (Fig. 15b), the Th-O bonds were not formed and, since the maximum values of the ultrasound parameters were used (Table 4), it seemed that the high intensity of these parameters resulted in the destruction of some of the bonds in the final structure [28]. In the present work, destruction of the bonds had a considerable effect on the thermal behaviour as well as adsorption/ desorption behaviours of the Th-MOF samples so that, in level b, the sample had the minimum thermal stability (Fig. 11) and surface area (Fig. 12). Moreover, adsorption/desorption behaviours of sample b was similar to the fourth-type of classical isotherms. On the other hand, in this level, since the surfactant had the minimum value compared with the optimal level, it could have no desirable effect on the properties of the sample b. Since ANOVA confirmed the importance of the interactions between the synthesis parameters, it was concluded that, in level $\pmb{b}$ , these interactions would intensify the effects of the high intensity of the ultrasound parameters (destruction of bonds) and the low contents of surfactant that caused a significant reduction in various properties of the sample [39]. In level $\pmb{g}$ , many of the synthesis parameters were similar to the optimal conditions. However, due to the shorter duration of irradiation, the particle size was slightly higher and the thermal stability and surface area were slightly less than the results obtained in the optimal level e. Moreover, the adsorption/desorption behaviours of the sample $\pmb{g}$ was similar to the optimal sample (similar to the fifth-type isotherm). In levels i and $\pmb{c}$ , since ultrasound duration and temperature had minimum values, the properties of Th-MOF samples were slightly reduced compared with the optimal conditions and the isothermal behaviours were similar to the first-type, which could be the reason for the micro distribution of the pores. In level $\pmb{c}$ , beside the low values of the ultrasound duration and temperature parameters, since the value of ultrasound power was higher than the optimal level (negative effect due to the high intensity), this factor caused a slight difference between samples c and i in terms of the obtained properties. FTIR spectra (Fig. 15) showed that the bonds associated with the formation of ThMOFs were formed properly in samples i and c. Based on the ANOVA results, the surfactant was selected as an important factor affecting the properties of Th-MOF samples. Therefore, it was expected that selecting its appropriate amount would lead to the improvement of various properties of the products [40]. However, in level $\pmb{h}$ , despite selecting the optimal values for the ultrasound parameters, since the surfactant’s value was lower than the optimal level, the thermal stability (Fig. 11) and surface area (Fig. 12) of sample $\pmb{h}$ were reduced. According to the SEM images (Fig. 10), the particles were partly aggregated together in level h. Compared with level $\pmb{h}$ , in level $\mathbf{\Delta}_{d}$ , the value of the surfactant was selected higher than the optimal value, causing the surfactant to react with the Th-MOF sample, instead of improving the properties of the product. As a result, the morphology of the sample $\textbf{\em d}$ was in the form of a rod (Fig. 10) with the average width of $123\mathrm{nm}$ , and its thermal stability and surface area were reduced compared with samples $\textbf{\em e},\textbf{\em g},\textbf{\em i},\textbf{\em c},$ and $\pmb{h}$ . TG and DSC analyses showed that, in sample d, the micelle disintegration temperature increased (Fig. 11), which could indicate the surfactant self-assembly. Fig. 15d shows the FTIR spectrum of sample d. According to this spectrum, the frequencies near $1092.9\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for $\scriptstyle{s=0}$ , and $694.2\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for $s{-}0$ bonds confirmed the occurrence of the surfactant self-assembly in level d. Based on the data derived from FTIR, the proposed structure for the surfactant self-assembly is shown in Fig. 16. In level $f,$ first, the value of the surfactant was lower than the optimal level (e); second, the values of ultrasound duration, and temperature were selected so high that the TheO bond (Fig. 15) was not formed properly (destroy the bond). Consequently, according to Fig. 10, these factors resulted in the formation of belts with the average width of $187\mathrm{nm}$ and aggregation of the particles due to the instability caused by the destruction of the $\mathrm{Th}{-}0$ bond in sample $f.$ Furthermore, according to Figs. 11 and 12, thermal stability and surface area of level $f$ were strictly reduced compared with the optimal level. In level $\mathbf{\alpha}_{\mathbf{a}}$ , as level $\pmb{d}$ , selecting high values of the surfactant led to the occurrence of the surfactant self-assembly [41]. However, since the parameters of ultrasound duration and power in level $\pmb{a}$ were selected at the minimum value compared with the optimal level and also due to the interaction between the factors (ANOVA results), thermal stability and surface area of sample $\pmb{a}$ were significantly reduced compared with sample d. in addition, the morphology of the product was such that the particles were agglomerated and their sizes were considerably increased. Fig. 17 shows the XRD patterns of the samples (a-i) under various synthesis conditions of UARM method. Based on these patterns, in level e, the product had wide peaks originated from the formation of Th-MOF sample in nanometric dimensions. In level b, the sample had an amorphous structure, which could be due to the destruction of the bonds caused by the high intensity of the ultrasound parameters as well as the low value of the surfactant (compared with the optimal level) [28]. In samples $\textbf{\em a}$ and $\pmb{d}$ , more peaks were observed compared with samples $c,i,e,f,g,$ and $\pmb{h}$ , indicating the surfactant self-assembly (consistent with the results of FTIR spectra). In level $f,$ due to selecting high values of ultrasound duration and temperature, some of the thorium MOF-related XRD patterns were not formed. However, in other levels, the XRD patterns related to the formation of Th-MOF were shown properly, except that based on the difference between the sizes of crystals, widening of the peaks was different from each other.
## 3.8. Optimization parameters
Based on the designs performed by $2^{\mathrm{K}-1}$ factorial and also the ANOVA results, the regression equations obtained for each property of the Th-MOF samples were as follows: SD (size distribution): $150+12.2$ $\mathrm{~A~+~61.7~~B~+~86.7~~C-2.1~D}$ , TS (thermal stability): $307+4.8$ ${\sf A}-15.2{\sf\Delta}{\sf B}+32.7{\sf C}-43.1$ D, and SA (surface area): $1158-514$ $\mathrm{A}-686\ \mathrm{B}-500\mathrm{C}+424\mathrm{D}$ . With regard to these equations, the surface plots were designed, which related the synthesis parameters to each of these properties. Considering the response surface plots, one can obtain the SD, TS, and SA values using each of the desirable values of surfactant content (A), ultrasound duration (B), temperature (C), and ultrasound power (D). In addition, Figs. 18–20 display surface plots for size distribution, thermal behaviour and surface area of Th-MOF samples obtained from regression models at A, B, C and $\pmb{D}$ factors. The top points of surface plots are optimum values. It is attractive to note that empirical experiments under similar process conditions (Table 4) are in good agreement with the estimated values. According to the obtained results (ANOVA, Pareto chart, and regression equations), RSM was used to optimize various properties of Th-MOF samples (Fig. 21). Since applying these compounds in various industrial fields required samples with high thermal stability, low size distribution, and high surface area, the objective of RSM was to predict the synthesis of Th-MOF samples with maximum thermal stability, minimum particle size, and maximum surface area. In this methodology, the values of the synthesis parameters for producing Th-MOF samples with such properties are provided as the outputs [42]. These properties as well as their predicted values are presented in a non-coded form in Table 5 (the formula of converting a code into non-coded form is presented in Table 3). With regard to these properties, the Th-MOF compounds can be expected to have numerous applicable capabilities in various industrial fields [43].
## 4. Conclusion
In this research, Th-MOF nanostructures were produced using conventional reverse micelle as well as ultrasound-assisted reverse micelle methodology. Analysis of the products of both methods by TG, X-ray and FTIR confirmed the formation of Th-MOF. However, interesting differences were observed in XRD patterns, TGA profiles and SEM images of the MOFs. In the case of the MOF synthesized using UARM, the XRD peaks were relatively broader, very smaller crystals were obtained and higher thermal stability was achieved. Additionally, SEM imaging showed very fine and non-agglomerated particles in the MOF synthesized using UARM technique. According to adsorption and desorption curves, UARM resulted in smaller pore size $(2.8\mathrm{nm})$ compared with RM $(0.7\mathrm{nm})$ . So, a Th-MOF with improved properties was produced which had high capability in different fields including high temperature services and catalysis. Experimental conditions such as surfactant concentration and ultrasound parameters were found to influence on the properties of the synthesized MOF. Probability plot did not show tangible orientation in residual and good normal distribution was obtained. ANOVA showed very good fit to quadratic model because of very high coefficient of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{\mathsf{R}}^{2}$ and very low $P_{\nu a l u e}(<0.010).$ . According to the RSM prediction on producing Th-MOF samples with high thermal stability, small particle size, and large surface area, it can be concluded the nanostructures synthesized in this research can be applied in various industrial fields.
